{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T12:24:21.134Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"24","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (24ca2ec6). CONFIRMED, and WORSE than reported: clearing the type scope did not merely let same-named nested declarations collide across classes — both nested helpers hashed to the SAME #sig:, separated only by a source-order ordinal, so adding a helper to one class silently moved the other's symbol ID. Fixed by keeping the scope and marking it callable (childScopeIsCallable), with lexicalCallableForm demoting method to function, giving 'function A.m.helper'. Empty-scope and arrow spellings stay byte-identical to main. Fail-without-fix: 'adding a nested helper to B.m moved A.m's helper'. Mutation: 7 total, one initially surviving and PROVEN a genuine escape via 'class A{m(){class C{static{function f(){}}}}}' — correct 'method C.f' versus the mutant's 'function C.f' — then killed by a third pin. TestNestedCallableScopeResetIsJavaScriptOnly, which pins the deliberate Python exclusion tracked in issue #199, stays green.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NMSYMHAT0D1KFMZZ04V3TE","targetId":"01M1NMSYMRA6YZ3TF3DT4KAQC5","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NCBTX43GVDZEMWYRW3KX4Y"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
